sequenceDiagram
  %% Container runtime (ADR 0250 step 8, ADR 0261, ADR 0266 slice 6, docs/reference/development-containers.md#sleep-and-wake, docs/reference/prepared-restore.md): read a sleeping development container's recorded pages back while it is still frozen, then resume it, or start one that was stopped. Defaults and nested-fleet Instances wake under the same rule as clones (owner decision 2026-11-14); a stopped nested-fleet Instance is never booted here. A request to its Route admits the same kind through the runtime activation endpoint of docs/reference/app-dev-runtime-hibernation.md#wake. Planned (ADR 0265, docs/reference/production-containers.md#sleep-and-wake): a production Instance's live release container wakes by the same rule, and its visitor is held as a development visitor is.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: instance:wake
  %% kind: instance.wake
  %% ADR 0266: signed power applies to ordinary wakes; the WebSocket is its only delivery, and only a stopped container continues in Swamp.
  autonumber
  %% lane CL icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CL as Caller<br/>orbit · Caddy
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>Instance container
  Note over GW,N: Signed Rust wake<br/>pool restore → Incus unfreeze
  %%> Executor proof path:
  %%> - a durable executor.power_proof Operation signs `wake`; the observer only relays bytes; the reply is `accepted`, then one result line
  %%> - before any memory syscall: boot id, cgroup inode and Incus incarnation against the plan, then each process's start time through a pidfd; any mismatch drops the plan and the wake goes on without a restore (`partial`, `instance.restore_plan_stale`)
  %%> - restore through the Node's one restore pool (`/etc/orbit/executor/restore-threads`, 32, floor 16), one target's items finished before the next starts: `process_madvise(MADV_WILLNEED)` then `process_vm_readv` of each recorded page that is not resident, within 2 s; at the bound the wake skips unstarted items and waits 250 ms for running ones, then reports `partial`, `instance.restore_deadline`; a read still stuck in its syscall then is stalled: `partial`, `instance.restore_stalled`, and the wake goes on, because a late read only copies into the executor's buffer; a later sleep or discard of the target waits up to 10 s for that reader, else `instance.restore_readers_busy`; a short read skips its failed page and continues with the rest of the item, counting unreadable pages; an unreadable pagemap or a failed page reports `partial`, `instance.restore_read_failed`; a plan that breaks a bound is `instance.restore_plan_invalid`
  %%> - unfreeze through the Incus local API with its 5 s bounds, then wait up to 5 s for the kernel thaw; a kernel freeze that stays, or one Incus lost before the wake, fails `instance.container_frozen_unmanaged` with no cgroup write
  %%> - close exactly the recorded swap limits, with no disk sync; one readiness request to the web port until an answer below 500, within 1.5 s, else `instance.web_unready` and the marker stays; then remove the asleep marker
  %%> - a stopped container fails `instance.container_stopped` and drops its plan: a boot is the run's work; a running one gets only the steps after the unfreeze (`done`), so crash recovery and a repeat are safe
  CL->>GW: instance:wake INSTANCE
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit instance:wake INSTANCE`
  %%> - syntax: `<instance-id> [--json]`
  %%> Callers:
  %%> - the command
  %%> - Caddy on the Instance's Node, when a request finds the asleep marker: `GET runtime-activations/app-instance/ID`
  %%> - a durable Conn launch that opens the T3 server; planned: the task flow before a turn on an attached Instance
  %%> - planned: a due native cron Schedule of a production Instance whose live sleeps; the fire waits for the wake, and this wake does not count as activity
  %%> - not needed by a copy: a clone or task copy of an asleep default copies its seed snapshot
  %%> - a deployment of a sleeping Instance resumes or starts it in its own run, and removes the marker there
  %%> - planned: a production deploy does not wake live; its switch removes the marker (container/deploy-production.mmd)
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the caller is the Instance's Node or has node access to it
  Note over GW: Start or join the wake<br/>one per Instance
  %%> When:
  %%> - an active development container, a default and a nested-fleet Instance included
  %%> - planned: a production Instance's live release container; the wake is admitted beside an open deploy or rollback, which changes live only after its switch; the switch and the wake take the Instance's power lock
  %%> - a nested-fleet Instance takes the wake only when its last sleep froze it and no wake since found it stopped; otherwise a nested-fleet clone takes its start (instance/start.mmd), and a nested-fleet default refuses: only its deployment starts it
  %%> Result:
  %%> - an open wake of the Instance is joined: a second caller gets the same Operation; a visit that joins is held on it too
  %%> - Caddy's visit is held while the wake runs, up to 30 s (`ORBIT_VISIT_WAKE_WAIT_SECONDS`): then `307` to the same URI, which the browser repeats to the awake app, or a plain `503` text with no page, script or reload (Nick 2026-11-19; supersedes the 2026-11-15 rule "wake spinner shows at once; never hold the request")
  %%> - a join to a Conn launch's wake records shared use while locking the wake Operation. If launch cleanup already cancelled the queued wake, a new request admits a new wake
  %%> - after a failed wake, the next visit admits a new wake; only an interrupted wake under its fence (R2) keeps answering its failure
  %%> On failure:
  %%> - no container, a non-development Instance, a nested-fleet Instance that cannot take the wake or its start, or other lifecycle work admits no wake
  alt signed wake admitted
    %% path: executor
    GW->>N: Sign and publish wake<br/>executor-order · WebSocket
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - record or join instance.wake under the Instance lock before signing; requests and the background pass share one publication receipt for the same Operation
    %%> - CLI, API and Conn queue the signed wake and follow the Operation; their wait is not limited by Caddy's request budget
    %%> - keep the admitted wake queued with zero attempts until the publisher atomically claims it; Conn records cancellation of an unshared queued executor wake in the launch cancel transaction, before a publisher can claim it
    %%> - sign and publish outside the database transaction under the non-blocking per-Operation publication lock, then record the publication receipt
    %%> - the background pass takes committed completion handoffs, including old queued handoffs whose jobs did not finish; it projects recorded results before publishing any missing order for the same Operation
    %%> - record Operation notices locally; delivering them never holds the visitor request
    %%> - the observer passes the order unread to the executor; the path needs no SSH, Python or marker/times command
    %%> - the WebSocket is the only delivery; the Gateway publishes once and never relays the order over SSH
    opt Caddy, for a visit
      GW->>GW: Hold the visit<br/>ExecutorResultWait · 30 s
      %%> Result:
      %%> - check the listener's subsecond receipt while time remains, because another waiter may have consumed its Valkey copy
      %%> - keep the observer's reply order; a bare replay refusal is not a result, and a late result cannot authorize the original request
      alt done or partial
        GW-->>CL: 307 to the same URI<br/>same method and body
        %%> Result:
        %%> - Caddy returns the 307 unchanged; the browser repeats the request and the awake app answers it. A 200 would end as an empty page on production's published routes, whose activation and app sit in separate `handle` blocks
        %%> - the Operation succeeds and records the runtime running
      else failed, refused or no result in 30 s
        %% path: executor-failed
        GW-->>CL: 503 plain text<br/>wake failed or still waking
        %%> Result:
        %%> - a failed or refused receipt, or an ended Operation, answers at once with its code; a reload admits a new wake
      end
    end
    alt a receipt arrives
      Note over GW: Project the receipt<br/>end the Operation
      %%> - R1: the matching receipt decides; done or partial succeeds, refused or failed fails with the receipt's code; a transport failure never overrides a recorded receipt
      %%> - R4: a visit answers a failed wake with a plain `503` and its code; under an interrupted wake's held lock every visit gets the same failure
      %%> - the worker sends the Operation's notices without waiting for the sweep
      %%> - the job after 6 s finishes a known result if the immediate job did not run
      %%> - a background worker runs the lifecycle hook after success without holding a database writer, and records it once per Operation
      opt the receipt is failed with instance.container_stopped
        GW->>SW: Continue Operation<br/>operation-instance-wake
        SW->>GW: Sign fresh order<br/>bound Operation
        SW->>N: Relay fresh order<br/>orbit-executor relay · stdin
        %%> - the run's phase on the Node is the relay's local caller; the journal returns the first result or acts once. A confirmed stopped container takes its existing boot and readiness phase; an unmanaged stopped nested default never boots here.
        %%> - After that boot, the boot's own readiness result decides, never the earlier `container_stopped` line (R1)
        %%> - R3: the engine that froze the container wakes it; a later cancelled or failed sleep and the current settings change nothing
        SW-->>GW: Return result<br/>terminal projection
      end
    else no result by 6 s
      %% path: journal
      GW->>N: Read journal<br/>SSH · read-only · this order
      %%> - covers a publish that threw, no `accepted` line and a lost result; none proves what the Node did, so the lock never goes on silence
      %%> - the command prints the Node's clock and the journal lines for this Operation, action and target; it sends no order and cannot act
      %%> - the executor records an order when it accepts it, before the target lock; an order that expired waiting ends `executor.order_expired` and never acts
      %%> - a `seen` or `open` record without `done` reads again after 1 s; each read is its own job, so an unreachable Node delays no other Instance
      alt a done record
        Note over GW: Project it as the receipt<br/>delivery journal
        %%> - R1: the record is the wake's outcome, success or failure, completed by the same code as an on-time receipt, also after an interruption (a stopped container still goes to the boot); the lock goes with it
      else no record, order expired on the Node
        %% path: not-accepted
        Note over GW: Fail, free the lock<br/>executor.not_accepted
        %%> - the Node never acted and never will; `executor.publish_failed` when the publish threw
        %%> - a held visit sees the failed Operation and gets the plain `503`; a reload admits a new wake
      else the Node cannot be read
        %% path: result-unknown
        Note over GW: Interrupted, keep lock<br/>executor.result_unknown
        %%> - R2: the executor may have acted, so the lock stays and cancel is refused; the Gateway reads the journal again every 10 s until a record or its absence settles it
        %%> - no Swamp run starts
      end
    end
  else Python wake admitted
  opt Caddy, for a development visit
    Note over GW: Hold the visit<br/>up to 30 s
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the Gateway admits or joins the wake, runs the Node's resume below within the visit, and waits for the Operation to end; `307` to the same URI, otherwise a plain `503`
    %%> Why:
    %%> - a container that Python froze wakes in 14–19 s until its next executor sleep; 30 s covers it without a second state
  end
  opt the last sleep froze it, not a nested fleet
    %% path: resume
    GW->>N: Write the wake request<br/>agent · hibernation.marker
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - the Operation is recorded without its run; the Node calls run after its commit
    %%> - a command caller and Caddy's held visit both wait for these calls
    %%> - the frozen agent's `hibernation.marker` writes `/dev/shm/orbit/hibernation/app-instance-<id>-op-<opid>.wake`; only root writes in that folder; the name carries the Operation, so no earlier wake's outcome can answer this one
    %%> When:
    %%> - the Node has the worker template `/etc/systemd/system/orbit-instance-wake@.service`, which only the role's convergence installs; the first read asks for it, and a Node without it skips this path for 10 minutes
    %%> - not for a nested-fleet Instance: its prepared fast path waits for a nested-fleet proof
    %%> Why:
    %%> - measured on a frozen clone: the run's start-up and playbook took 8-13 s of the 9-19 s a visitor waited
    %%> - the agent stays frozen (ADR 0253): two of its existing commands carry the request and the answer
    Note over N: Restore, then resume<br/>orbit-instance-wake@ID-OP
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - a path unit's dispatcher renames the request to `.taken` and starts the worker for this Instance and Operation; a slow worker never holds up the next request
    %%> - the worker acts only on a request under 1 s old, with the asleep marker and a valid target descriptor; it runs `orbit-instance-power restore` as the unit `orbit-instance-power-<id>`, the Instance's lock
    %%> - restore, still frozen: checks boot id, incarnation, cgroup and every process's identity before any memory call, then `process_madvise` and `process_vm_readv` over the recorded pages in parallel, within 2 s; then one `incus resume`, and swap closes on every cgroup of `limits.json`
    %%> - then one request from the Node to the web port for an answer below 500, removes the marker, writes one `.ready-complete`, `-partial`, `-skipped` or `-timed-out` and then `.done` in `/var/lib/orbit/hibernation/outcomes`
    %%> - last, the library cache and CA bundle checks the run also makes
    %%> Why:
    %%> - frozen processes cannot change their mappings, so the plan stays exact while it is read
    %%> - the pages come back in large parallel reads, not one fault at a time after the resume
    %%> On failure:
    %%> - a changed or missing plan: no memory call; an ordinary resume, `.ready-skipped`
    %%> - a declined request, a busy lock, a failed resume or check: `.failed`, the marker stays
    GW->>N: Read the outcome<br/>agent · hibernation.times
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - reads the request, `.taken`, the outcome files and the marker every 25 ms, for at most 6 s
    %%> Result:
    %%> - `.done` with one `.ready-*`: the Operation succeeds here with `wake: resumed`, `trigger_seconds`, `confirm_seconds`, `restore_mode`, `ready_at` from the ready file's time (`ready_at_resolution_seconds` 1) and `prepared_restore.status`; `power_state` is `running`; Conn's renewal below runs as after a run
    %%> - after the projection, the Gateway removes this Operation's request and outcome files off the visitor's path
    %%> - Caddy's held visit gets 200 and the app's own page
    %%> - an API caller gets 202 with the ended Operation; a command caller who joins waits up to the bound; a visit that joins is held until the wake ends
    %%> On failure:
    %%> - `.failed`, an agent call that failed or the bound: the Gateway removes the request, and the same Operation starts the run below; the run takes the same lock, so it waits for a worker still at work and goes on from the state the Node reached
    %%> - a Gateway worker that dies here: a delayed start runs the same Operation's run 15 s later
    %%> - a cancel during the resume keeps the Operation cancelled, and a late outcome never overwrites it
  end
  opt Caddy, for a production visit, planned
    GW-->>CL: Admitted, at once<br/>Caddy holds · bound 10 s
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - the Gateway admits or joins the wake and answers at once, as in development; no Gateway worker waits for the wake
    %%> - Caddy on the Node holds the visitor's request: it tries the live upstream again until the asleep marker is gone, within 10 s, and then proxies the request to live
    %%> - a person, an API client or a webhook sees only a slower first answer; targets about 1.5 s with the prepared restore, 2.4 s without
    %%> - the wake reads the live link after the power lock and acts only on that container; after a switch it finds no marker, `wake: already_running`
    %%> Why:
    %%> - a page instead of the app's answer breaks an API client and a webhook (ADR 0265)
    %%> On failure:
    %%> - after 10 s, Caddy answers `503` with `Retry-After: 5` and a plain page that names the Instance; the wake goes on
    %%> - a failure is stored as in development; while it is stored, the Gateway says so and Caddy answers `503` with `Retry-After: 30` and the same page at once
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - the first request 30 s or more after the failure starts one new wake by itself: at most one retry each 30 s
    %%> - `instance:wake` retries at once
    %%> - when the fast wake fell back to the run below, the hold goes on until the run ends or the bound
  end
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-wake
  GW-->>CL: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation ID
  SW->>N: Wake the container<br/>Ansible · tags start
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - Frozen: `orbit-instance-power restore` as the Instance's lock unit, a busy lock (systemd's `UnitExists` refusal) retried 0.25 s apart, 40 times, else `instance.power_helper_busy`; a helper crash fails with `instance.power_helper_failed`: with a valid plan it reads the recorded pages back within 2 s while still frozen; then one `incus resume`, and `memory.swap.max` back to 0 on each inner cgroup of `limits.json` that still exists, and on the container's own cgroup back to its `limits.memory.swap` bytes, or 0 without a size (Incus gives 0 without a size; a nested-fleet container keeps its swap) (a short-lived unit's cgroup may be gone; that never fails the wake); FrankenPHP gets 10 tries 1 s apart from the Node to its address; nothing boots and nothing converges, `wake: resumed`
  %%> - a missing, stale or invalid plan makes no memory call; the resume is ordinary
  %%> - a run that follows the fast path finds the state the Node reached: a container that already runs is not resumed again, and the run reads this Operation's outcome files for their facts
  %%> - a nested-fleet Instance resumes with its inner fleet, `wg-nested` and the Club key in /run: a freeze keeps them, so the wake starts and checks no fleet
  %%> - Frozen and FrankenPHP silent: `incus stop --force`, then the boot below, `wake: restarted_after_resume`; a nested-fleet Instance is never stopped: the wake fails `instance.web_unready`
  %%> - Stopped (after a host reboot or a sleep that fell back to a stop): the helper discards the plan, then the boot below, `wake: started`; a nested-fleet Instance is never booted here: see the branch below
  %%> - Running (after a reboot with `boot.autostart`): no start and no resume, `wake: already_running`
  %%> - a Frozen container without the marker is resumed; the result says `marker_found: false`
  %%> - before the boot, the presented machine name: a stopped container gets `/etc/hostname`, `/etc/machine-info` and the `127.0.1.1` line of `/etc/hosts` through the file API before the boot, so the boot gives the kernel and T3 the name; a running or frozen container gets nothing, and nothing restarts
  %%> - boot: `incus start`; nftables, T3, FrankenPHP and the enabled Process units start with the container
  %%> - an older container's app account moves to the stock `www-data` (uid and gid 33) as soon as it runs, before any other command in it, by root's one swap file `/usr/local/libexec/orbit-app-user`; a moved container only reads, and a nested-fleet container keeps `orbit` and `work` for its inner fleet, with `www-data` in `work`
  %%> - waits until each desired-running unit is active, then until FrankenPHP answers, else `instance.web_unready`
  %%> - a 5xx answer gets up to 5 s to clear; an app that answers 5xx by itself is then served as it is
  %%> - removes the asleep marker, so Caddy sends the next request to the app
  %%> - then rebuilds the container's library cache with `ldconfig -X` when it lacks a soname of the runtime folder (a converge cannot exec in a frozen container); a failed check fails nothing
  %%> - then runs `update-ca-certificates` when the CA bundle lacks the container's Orbit root (the converge writes the root's file, but a frozen container refuses the exec); a container without the file skips it
  %%> - then waits up to 30 s for T3's environment endpoint in an Instance with a T3 server, else `instance.t3_unready`; a default has none and ends with the app
  %%> Why:
  %%> - one playbook: a second Ansible start costs about 4 s on the visitor's path
  %%> - the visitor waits for the app, not for T3; T3 gates only the Operation's end
  %%> - unit enablement holds each Process's desired state, so a stopped Process stays stopped
  %%> When:
  %%> - the wake's state handling runs only in a start whose intent names `marker`; a deployment's start and a nested-fleet clone's start name `asleep_marker` instead, which only removes the marker once the `start` part passed, with no web probe
  alt the run passes
    %% path: main
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal result
    Note over GW: Record awake
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the runtime's `power_state` is `running`; the Conn layer shows the T3 server awake
    %%> - the result names the path, `marker_found`, and a resume's `resume_seconds` and `web_probe_seconds`
    %%> - `restore_mode` (`prepared` or `ordinary`), `prepared_restore` with its status and reason, and `ready_at`, when the marker went (`ready_at_resolution_seconds` 0.001); Operation list and show return them
    %%> - a held visit gets 200 and the app answers its original path
    opt the Gateway session ends within 7 days or has ended
      Note over GW: Admit the renewal<br/>conn.environment_renew
      %%> When:
      %%> - after the wake's transaction; it never fails the wake
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - see conn/environment-renew.mmd; the run needs no old session, so a session that ended during the sleep renews too
    end
    opt a held session is due or owes a revoke
      Note over GW: Renew held sessions<br/>owed revoke next
      %%> When:
      %%> - after the wake's transaction, while the Gateway session has not ended; it never fails the wake
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - see conn/session-hold.mmd: one renewal per held session that ends within seven days
      %%> - and the server's next owed held revoke (conn/pairing-revoke.mmd), which waited while the Instance slept
    end
  else T3 does not answer
    %% path: t3-unready
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>instance.t3_unready
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the Operation fails; the marker is gone and the Route serves the app
    %%> - the runtime's `power_state` is `running`, because the container runs
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - the same command; a start of a running container changes nothing
  else a nested-fleet container is stopped
    %% path: nested-stopped
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>instance.nested_fleet_stopped
    %%> Why:
    %%> - a host reboot or a stop ended the freeze; a boot without the Club key and `wg-nested` gives a fleet that cannot pass Doctor
    %%> Result:
    %%> - nothing boots; the marker stays and `power_state` stays `stopped`
    %%> - the sleep no longer counts as frozen: the next wake of a clone takes its start, which removes the marker; a default starts only by its deployment
  else a step fails before the app answers
    %% path: failed
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step, error_code
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the asleep marker stays; a held visit gets a plain `503`: the wake failed, with its code
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - a reload of the page or the same command; a start of a running container changes nothing
    %%> - planned, a production live: the first visit 30 s or more after the failure starts one new wake by itself; until then Caddy answers `503` with `Retry-After: 30`
  end
  end
  loop until terminal
    CL->>GW: operation:show TASK
    GW-->>CL: Status and result
  end
